{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Concurrent reducer cache writers can publish a current hash with stale state\n\n## Context and authoritative intent\n\nThe reducer cache is a rebuildable optimization over deterministic event replay. `write_cache` promises an atomic cache publication, the cache invalidation tests require a newly added event to be reflected, and the original plan-review fan-out epic `sulky-pane-flag` requires concurrency without fidelity regression.\n\nThe report originally attributed a cap=12 plan-review abort to the `.cache.json.tmp` rename race. Phase-1 experiments rejected that fatality claim for immutable gate snapshots: redundant writers log caught warnings but do not escape or corrupt the equal result. The confirmed defect is reachable when concurrent reductions observe different event generations.\n\n## Proven root cause\n\n`src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py` gives every writer the same `.cache.json.tmp` path. Overlapping writers can hold file descriptors to the same inode; after one renames it into place, another can keep mutating the now-visible final file. Runtime proof through real `reduce_ticket` appended an EDIT between old-generation and new-generation reductions and produced a valid cache with the current hash but stale `authorship`. A subsequent normal reduction returned the stale cached state without replay. Serialized controls produced no false hits.\n\n## Repair plan\n\n1. Write each cache envelope to a private same-directory dot-temp file, close it completely, then atomically replace `.cache.json`; always clean up the private temp on failure. Preserve the current best-effort/non-fatal cache-I/O behavior and add no dependency.\n2. Extend the canonical tracker gitignore ensure-registry so private cache temp names cannot be staged by concurrent `git add -A`; pin that registry/staging contract.\n3. Add a deterministic threaded reducer regression: capture coherent old/new states around a controlled event append, release at least eight writers through the real cache seam, and assert the current-hash read equals a cache-bypassing replay oracle. Also assert identical concurrent writers emit no temp-rename failures after repair.\n4. Run the focused reducer/cache and init/gitignore suites, then lint and typecheck.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Concurrent reductions of different event generations can publish only complete coherent envelopes; a current-hash cache hit equals cache-bypassing deterministic replay.\n- [ ] At least eight concurrent reductions of one immutable ticket complete without `FileNotFoundError` cache-write warnings.\n- [ ] Each writer uses an isolated same-directory temp artifact and failure cleanup leaves no temp files.\n- [ ] Every cache temp artifact shape is covered by the canonical tracker gitignore/ensure-registry contract and cannot be staged by `git add -A`.\n- [ ] Cache I/O remains best-effort: publication failure is logged and does not prevent the independently replayed ticket state from returning.\n- [ ] Focused tests, `make lint`, and `make typecheck` pass."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786302660519728001,"uuid":"22c47547-bfdf-40e8-a742-ab14ff8b8350"}